-X11 applications have no per-output scale. By default they see every output at its logical size, and Umbriel upscales their windows on outputs with a scale above 1, which looks soft. With `xwayland_native_resolution = true` they see each output at its physical size instead: a `3840x2160` output at scale `1.25` appears to them as `3840x2160` rather than `3072x1728`. Their windows render at one pixel per screen pixel and stay sharp, but X11 applications that do not scale their own interface look smaller on scaled outputs. Games benefit most.
+X11 applications have no per-output scale. By default they see every output at its logical size, and Umbriel upscales their windows on outputs with a scale above 1, which looks soft. With `xwayland_native_resolution = true` they see each output at its rendered size instead — its `render_scale`, or its scale when `render_scale` is unset: a `3840x2160` output at scale `1.25` appears to them as `3840x2160` rather than `3072x1728`. Their windows render at one pixel per screen pixel and stay sharp, but X11 applications that do not scale their own interface look smaller on scaled outputs. Games benefit most.
 
