From 05ac404a2e2e5ae98914104d4917949ca2b92e6e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ZhangDM-520 <jixianqiang1540@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 09:55:07 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 3/3] fix(review): pin mirror isolation and harden the
 render_scale checks
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

External-review follow-ups on the render_scale change:

- F1: add umbrielfx/tests/scale_hints.c — one provider scene and one
  render-only mirror scene sharing a surface across two outputs. Pins that
  the mirror keeps membership but publishes no hints, and that the
  provider's derivation excludes outputs whose answer is non-positive even
  when their raw scale is highest. Mutation-proven (gate removal and
  exclusion leak both go red).
- F2: protocol/render_scale phase 4 now count-polls for a fresh mapped line
  before asserting, so a stale line cannot pass vacuously. A same-scale
  re-notify is deduped below the wire (verified: the reveal re-presents but
  emits no new hint event), so the fresh presentation is the observable
  artifact. Probe: removing the round-trip fails the count check.
- F3: lock the capture-source contract in capture.c: the synthetic output's
  `data` stays NULL so shells can exclude capture mirrors from hints.
- F5: note that the undersample warning cannot see auto-detected scales.

Co-authored-by: Copilot <223556219+Copilot@users.noreply.github.com>
---
 docs/user/outputs.md                          |   2 +-
 tests/harness/checks/protocol/render_scale.sh |  27 ++--
 umbrielfx/meson.build                         |  16 +++
 umbrielfx/tests/scale_hints.c                 | 125 ++++++++++++++++++
 umbrielfx/types/scene/capture.c               |   4 +
 5 files changed, 164 insertions(+), 10 deletions(-)
 create mode 100644 umbrielfx/tests/scale_hints.c

diff --git a/docs/user/outputs.md b/docs/user/outputs.md
index f686ba2b..65da9d68 100644
--- a/docs/user/outputs.md
+++ b/docs/user/outputs.md
@@ -115,7 +115,7 @@ Omit `position` to place outputs automatically from left to right. Explicitly po
 
 `render_scale` separates what things look like from what clients render at, the way macOS handles HiDPI. With `scale = 1.35` and `render_scale = 2.0`, clients are asked to render at 2.0 and the compositor samples their buffers down to the output's `scale` raster, which smooths text and fine detail on low-ppi and non-RGB-subpixel panels. Logical geometry is unchanged: windows keep their `scale` sizes, and the output still advertises `scale` so client interfaces size as before.
 
-Applications that ignore the scale hints render as they always have. Values below `scale` are honored but undersample, which looks blurry. Hint-following buffers sized at `render_scale` no longer match the output mode while `render_scale` differs from `scale`, so they do not qualify for [direct scanout](#direct-scanout); set `render_scale` equal to `scale` for scanout-sensitive games.
+Applications that ignore the scale hints render as they always have. Values below `scale` are honored but undersample, which looks blurry. The configuration warning compares `render_scale` against the configured `scale` (1.0 when `scale` is unset), so it cannot see an auto-detected output scale. Hint-following buffers sized at `render_scale` no longer match the output mode while `render_scale` differs from `scale`, so they do not qualify for [direct scanout](#direct-scanout); set `render_scale` equal to `scale` for scanout-sensitive games.
 
 Larger client buffers cost memory and sampling work: at `render_scale = 2.0` with `scale = 1.35` they hold about 2.2 times as many pixels as at `scale` alone, growing with the square of the ratio. Prefer whole or half-integer values for crisp buffer ratios.
 
